  "qualification": {
    "memory_evidence": "MEASURED here [M], three runs, by sampled `footprint phys_footprint_peak` in the process doing the work: 600 tokens -> 24.0 s of 48 kHz stereo audio, 58.6 s end to end, peak 10.79 GB; 900 tokens through the runner bridge -> 36.0 s, 103.2 s, peak 11.81 GB (receipt qualification/yue2-music-results-20260927/bridge-run.json); 3000 tokens -> 120.0 s, 429.8 s, peak 11.93 GB. The peak barely moves with length because the weights dominate: 2.66 GB 8-bit AR + 2.93 GB bf16 NAR + 0.51 GB VAE. A 16 GB Mac therefore has roughly 4 GB left for macOS, and the port's own monitor defaults to a 16 GiB process budget and fails closed on more than 64 MiB of new swap. An 8 GB Mac cannot run it in any configuration this port offers: the AR is the only quantizable part, and 4-bit would save about 1 GB against an 11.9 GB peak.",
    "minimum_unified_memory_gb": 16,
    "reason": "MEASURED by three local runs on the owner's 32 GB M2 Max through the port's own pipeline at the pinned commit 9253ed133343406947bde7b67d43c7a63fb39d99, two of them through this app's runner bridge, with the peak taken by sampled `footprint` in the process doing the work rather than from MLX's own allocator (which reports about half). What is NOT covered and is not claimed: no 16 GB or 24 GB machine has run it - the tier is a floor from a host under no memory pressure, and the 16 GB verdict is the owner's run to make. The port's own README targets a 16 GiB process budget and states it was tested on an M3 Max (128 GB) and an M5 Air (32 GB); it publishes no memory table of its own, so the numbers above are the only measured ones that exist.",
    "recommended_unified_memory_gb": 24,
    "status": "qualified"
  },
